  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nToday should be mainly a pleasant day across southwest Kansas.\nMid to high level moisture will continue advect in from the\nsouthern Rockies today. PWATs will continue to increase ahead of\na mid-level disturbance through early evening. As this disturbance\nmoves closer to the FA, do expect increasing areal coverage of \nshowers and thunderstorms to spread across far western and \nsouthwestern Kansas this afternoon and evening. CAMs like the \nHRRR, 3 km NAM, and ARW are in general agreement with this \nprecipitation potential later on today and tonight. The highest \nPOPs will be relegated to this area. For areas farther east, \nsuch as Dodge City, Hays, and Pratt... a mainly dry day is \nforecast with precipitation not entering the picture until after\nWednesday. Otherwise, highs today will range from the mid 70s \nin the showery areas, to 80s elsewhere in the more drier weather\npattern.\n\nThis main widespread soaking rainfall is not expected until the\nThursday and Friday timeframe. Here, PWATs will continue to\nincrease even farther with values ranging in the 1.50-1.75\"\nrange. This is well above the 90th percentile, with some areas\nnearing near the max statistical mark. Forecast LREF probabilities\nfor storm total greater than 0.25\" really start to peak over \n90% from the west to the east through Friday evening. For \namounts greater than 0.75\", these higher LREF probabilities 50% \nto 75% are mainly kept across far western and southwestern \nKansas. This agrees with WPC excessive rainfall outlook, with \nthe highest probabilities (slight risk) across far western and \nsouthwest Kansas Thursday, and then more probabilities (marginal\nrisk) spreading east Friday. Examining ensemble members from \nboth the GEFS and ENS... do echo this pattern with the wettest \nsolutions across the far southwest and western zones, and with \nensemble rain amounts then lowering farther east. The latest \nofficial forecast has rain amounts around or higher than 1\" for \nElkhart, Liberal and Garden City. Rainfall amounts of 0.25 to 1\"\nare forecast for the rest of the communities across southwest, \ncentral, and south-central Kansas. Isolated higher amounts of \ncourse cannot be ruled out at this time. This will depend on \nexact position of the storms, if there is any training \noccurring, and just exactly how strong storm strength will be \ngiven at any particularly time. Please continue to keep updated\nwith the forecast, as these amounts will surely change over the\nnext few days.\n\nLooking past this wet period, the global models and their\nensemble counterparts start to forecast drier air advecting in \nfrom the west along with shortwave ridging over the weekend. \nThis would support the notion for a drier and warmer forecast \nover the weekend. Generally speaking, it looks like temperatures\nwill be above normal right in time for the weekend. Beyond \nthis, ensembles are indicating for more unsettled weather next \nweek with more disturbances and moisture coming in from the \nsouthwestern United States.",
